.Fl s Cm help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a hypervisor that runs guest operating systems inside a
virtual machine.
It can run guests on amd64 and arm64 platforms with suitable hardware support.
.Pp
Parameters such as the number of virtual CPUs, amount of guest memory, and
I/O connectivity can be specified with command-line parameters.
.Pp
.Nm
is typically used with a boot ROM that can load the guest operating system.
On arm64 platforms, this is currently required.
If not using a boot ROM, the guest operating system must be loaded with
.Xr bhyveload 8
or a similar boot loader before running
.Nm ,
otherwise.
On amd64, the
.Pa edk2-bhyve
package provides a UEFI firmware that can be used to boot the guest;
on arm64 the
.Pa u-boot-bhyve-arm64
package provides a U-Boot image that can be used to boot the guest.
.Pp
.Nm
runs until the guest operating system reboots (if
.Ql monitor
mode is not enabled) or halts, or an unhandled hypervisor exit is
detected.
.Pp
Generally
.Nm
must be run by the super-user, but users belonging to the
.Va vmm
group can create and run virtual machines as well.
See
.Xr vmm 4 .
When run by an unprivileged user,
.Nm
must have access to any required resources such as disk images or
network devices.
PCI passthrough cannot be used by unprivileged users.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Fl a
The guest's local APIC is configured in xAPIC mode.
This option only applies to the amd64 platform.
xAPIC mode is the default setting so this option is redundant.
It will be deprecated in a future version.
.It Fl C
Include guest memory in core files.
.It Fl c Oo Oo Cm cpus= Oc Ns Ar numcpus Oc Ns Oo Cm ,sockets= Ns Ar n Oc Ns Oo Cm ,cores= Ns Ar n Oc Ns Oo Cm ,threads= Ns Ar n Oc
Number of guest virtual CPUs
and/or the CPU topology.
The default value for each of
.Ar numcpus ,
.Ar sockets ,
.Ar cores ,
and
.Ar threads
is 1.
If
.Ar numcpus
is not specified then it will be calculated from the other arguments.
The topology must be consistent in that the
.Ar numcpus
must equal the product of
.Ar sockets ,
.Ar cores ,
and
.Ar threads .
If a
.Ar setting
is specified more than once the last one has precedence.
.Pp
The maximum number of virtual CPUs defaults to the number of active
physical CPUs in the system available via the
.Va hw.vmm.maxcpu
.Xr sysctl 8
variable.
The limit can be adjusted via the
.Va hw.vmm.maxcpu
loader tunable.
.It Fl D
Destroy the VM on guest initiated power-off.
.It Fl e
Force
.Nm
to exit when a guest issues an access to an I/O port that is not emulated.
This is intended for debug purposes and only applies to the amd64 platform.
.It Fl f Ar name Ns Cm \&, Ns Oo Cm string Ns No | Ns Cm file Ns Oc Ns Cm \&= Ns Ar data
Add a fw_cfg file
.Ar name
to the fw_cfg interface.
If a
.Cm string
is specified, the fw_cfg file contains the string as data.
If a
.Cm file
is specified, bhyve reads the file and adds the file content as fw_cfg data.
.It Fl G Xo
.Sm off
.Oo Cm w Oc
.Oo Ar bind_address Cm \&: Oc
.Ar port
.Sm on
.Xc
Start a debug server that uses the GDB protocol to export guest state to a
debugger.
An IPv4 TCP socket will be bound to the supplied
.Ar bind_address
and
.Ar port
to listen for debugger connections.
Only a single debugger may be attached to the debug server at a time.
If the option begins with
.Cm w ,
.Nm
will pause execution at the first instruction waiting for a debugger to attach.
.It Fl H
Yield the virtual CPU thread when a HLT instruction is detected.
If this option is not specified, virtual CPUs will use 100% of a host CPU.
This option applies only to the amd64 platform.
.It Fl h
Print help message and exit.
.It Fl k Ar config_file
Set configuration variables from a simple, key-value config file.
Each line of the config file is expected to consist of a config variable
name, an equals sign
.Pq Sq = ,
and a value.
No spaces are permitted between the variable name, equals sign, or
value.
Blank lines and lines starting with
.Sq #
are ignored.
See
.Xr bhyve_config 5
for more details.
.It Fl K Ar layout
Specify the keyboard layout.
The value that can be specified sets the file name in
.Ar /usr/share/bhyve/kbdlayout .
This specification only works when loaded with UEFI mode for VNC.
When using a VNC client that supports QEMU Extended Key Event Message (e.g.
TigerVNC), this option isn't needed.
When using a VNC client that doesn't support QEMU Extended Key Event Message
(e.g. TightVNC), the layout defaults to the US keyboard unless specified
otherwise.
.It Fl l Cm help
Print a list of supported LPC devices.
.It Fl l Ar lpcdev Ns Op Cm \&, Ns Ar conf
Allow devices behind the LPC PCI-ISA bridge to be configured.
The only supported devices are the TTY-class devices
.Cm com1 , com2 , com3 ,
and
.Cm com4 ,
the TPM module
.Cm tpm ,
the boot ROM device
.Cm bootrom ,
the
.Cm fwcfg
type and the debug/test device
.Cm pc-testdev .
.Pp
The possible values for the
.Ar conf
argument are listed in the
.Fl s
flag description.
.Pp
This option applies only to the amd64 platform.
On arm64, the console and boot ROM devices are configured using the
more generic
.Fl o
option.
.It Xo
.Fl m Ar memsize Ns Oo
.Sm off
.Cm K | k | M | m | G | g | T | t
.Sm on
.Oc
.Xc
Set the guest physical memory size.
This must be the same size that was given to
.Xr bhyveload 8 .
.Pp
The size argument may be suffixed with one of
.Cm K , M , G
or
.Cm T
(either upper or lower case)
to indicate a multiple of kilobytes, megabytes, gigabytes, or terabytes.
If no suffix is given, the value is assumed to be in megabytes.
The default is 256M.
.It Fl M
Run the VM in
.Ql monitor
mode.
In this mode, a guest reboot does not cause the bhyve process to exit.
Instead, bhyve will restart the VM.
Once the bhyve process exits or is killed, the VM will be destroyed automatically.
.Pp
.It Fl n Ar id Ns Cm \&, Ns Ar size Ns Cm \&, Ns Ar cpus Ns Op Cm \&, Ns Ar domain_policy
Configure guest NUMA domains.
This option applies only to the amd64 platform.
.Pp
The
.Fl n
option allows the guest physical address space to be partitioned into domains.
The layout of each domain is encoded in an ACPI table
visible to the guest operating system.
The
.Fl n
option also allows the specification of a
.Xr domainset 9
memory allocation policy for the host memory backing a given NUMA domain.
A guest can have up to 8 NUMA domains.
This feature requires that the guest use a boot ROM, and in
particular cannot be used if the guest was initialized using
.Xr bhyveload 8 .
.Pp
Each domain is identified by a numerical
.Em id .
The domain memory
.Em size
is specified using the same format as the
.Fl m
flag.
The sum of all
.Em size
parameters overrides the total VM memory size specified by the
.Fl m
flag.
However, if at least one domain memory size parameter is
missing, the total VM memory size will be distributed across all
emulated domains in whole host-page units.
The resulting sizes differ by at most one host page and their sum is
exactly the configured total.
The
.Em cpuset
parameter specifies the set of CPUs that are part of the domain.
The
.Em domain_policy
parameter may be optionally used to configure the
.Xr domainset 9
host NUMA memory allocation policy for an emulated
domain.
See the
.Ar -n
flag in
.Xr cpuset 1
for a list of valid NUMA memory allocation policies and their formats.
.It Fl o Ar var Ns Cm = Ns Ar value
Set the configuration variable
.Ar var
to
.Ar value .
See
.Xr bhyve_config 5
for configuration options.
.It Fl P
Force the guest virtual CPU to exit when a PAUSE instruction is detected.
This option applies only to the amd64 platform.
.It Fl p Ar vcpu Ns Cm \& : Ns Ar hostcpu
Pin guest's virtual CPU
.Em vcpu
to
.Em hostcpu .
Host CPUs and guest virtual CPUs are numbered starting from 0.
A
.Fl p
option is required for every guest vCPU to be pinned.
To map a 4 vCPU guest to host CPUs 12-15:
.Bd -literal
-p 0:12 -p 1:13 -p 2:14 -p 3:15
.Ed
.It Fl r Ar file
Resume a guest from a snapshot.
For current snapshots,
.Ar file
is a manifest naming a generation-matched guest-memory file, kernel/device
state file, and metadata file in the same directory.
The manifest is published atomically only after all three members have been
durably written.
A sibling
.Dq Ar file Ns .lock
serializes checkpoint replacement with restore while the referenced members
are opened.
Current manifests include unkeyed SHA-256 values for each member and verify
them before publishing restored state.
These values detect accidental or post-publication changes but do not
authenticate an untrusted checkpoint producer.
The manifest, its members, and their containing directory must therefore be
controlled by a trusted operator.
Legacy snapshots in which
.Ar file
contains guest memory and
.Dq Ar file Ns .kern
contains state remain readable.
Version-2 manifests also remain readable and do not contain member digests.
.Pp
Note that the current snapshot file format requires that the
configuration of devices in the new VM match the VM from which the
snapshot was taken by specifying the same
.Fl s
and
.Fl l
options.
The count of vCPUs and memory configuration are read from the snapshot.
.It Fl S
Wire guest memory.
.It Fl s Cm help
Print a list of supported PCI devices.
.It Fl s Ar slot Ns Cm \&, Ns Ar emulation Ns Op Cm \&, Ns Ar conf
Configure a virtual PCI slot and function.
.Pp
.Nm
provides PCI bus emulation and virtual devices that can be attached to
slots on the bus.
There are 32 available slots, with the option of providing up to 8 functions
per slot.
.Pp
The
.Ar slot
can be specified in one of the following formats:
.Pp
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Ar pcislot
.It
.Sm off
.Ar pcislot Cm \&: Ar function
.Sm on
.It
.Sm off
.Ar bus Cm \&: Ar pcislot Cm \&: Ar function
.Sm on
.El
.Pp
The
.Ar pcislot
value is 0 to 31.
The optional
.Ar function
value is 0 to 7.
The optional
.Ar bus
value is 0 to 255.
If not specified, the
.Ar function
value defaults to 0.
If not specified, the
.Ar bus
value defaults to 0.
.Pp
See
.Sx "PCI EMULATION"
for available options for the
.Ar emulation
argument.
.It Fl U Ar uuid
Set the universally unique identifier
.Pq UUID
in the guest's System Management BIOS System Information structure.
By default a UUID is generated from the host's hostname and
.Ar vmname .
.It Fl u
RTC keeps UTC time.
.It Fl W
Force virtio PCI device emulations to use MSI interrupts instead of MSI-X
interrupts.
.It Fl w
Ignore accesses to unimplemented Model Specific Registers (MSRs).
This is intended for debug purposes.
.It Fl x
The guest's local APIC is configured in x2APIC mode.
This option applies only to the amd64 platform.
.It Fl Y
Disable MPtable generation.
This option applies only to the amd64 platform.
.It Ar vmname
Alphanumeric name of the guest.
This should be the same as that created by
.Xr bhyveload 8 .
.El
.Sh PCI EMULATION
.Nm
provides emulation for various PCI devices.
They are specified by the
.Fl s
.Ar slot,emulation,conf
configuration's
.Ar emulation
argument, which can be one of the following:
.Bl -tag -width "amd_hostbridge"
.It Cm hostbridge
A simple host bridge.
This is usually configured at slot 0, and is required by most guest
operating systems.
.It Cm amd_hostbridge
Emulation identical to
.Cm hostbridge
using a PCI vendor ID of AMD.
.It Cm passthru
PCI pass-through device.
.It Cm virtio-net
Virtio network interface.
The optional
.Cm transport
setting accepts
.Cm legacy
or
.Cm modern .
If omitted, the legacy PCI transport and its existing device identity are used
for compatibility with existing guests and launch scripts.
The modern setting selects the non-transitional VirtIO PCI transport and
device ID 0x1041.
For modern devices,
.Cm packed=true
enables the explicitly selected packed-virtqueue implementation.
It is disabled by default.
.It Cm virtio-blk
Virtio block storage interface.
The optional
.Cm transport
setting accepts
.Cm legacy
or
.Cm modern .
If omitted, the legacy PCI transport and its existing device identity are used
for compatibility with existing guests and launch scripts.
The modern setting selects the non-transitional VirtIO PCI transport and
device ID 0x1042.
For modern devices, the optional
.Cm queues
setting accepts a value from 1 through 8.
Values greater than 1 advertise
.Dv VIRTIO_BLK_F_MQ
and create that many independent request virtqueues.
The default is one queue.
For modern devices,
.Cm packed=true
enables packed virtqueues and is disabled by default.
Modern block devices also advertise
.Dv VIRTIO_BLK_F_CONFIG_WCE .
They retain the historical writeback default, permit the guest to select
writethrough mode, and return to writeback mode on device reset.
.It Cm virtio-scsi
Virtio SCSI interface.
The optional
.Cm transport
setting accepts
.Cm legacy
or
.Cm modern .
If omitted, the legacy PCI transport and its existing device identity are used
for compatibility with existing guests and launch scripts.
The modern setting selects the non-transitional VirtIO PCI transport and
device ID 0x1048.
For modern devices, the optional
.Cm queues
setting accepts a value from 1 through 8 and creates that many independent
request virtqueues.
Values greater than one require
.Cm transport=modern .
The default is one queue.
For modern devices,
.Cm packed=true
enables packed virtqueues and is disabled by default.
.It Cm virtio-9p
Virtio 9p (VirtFS) interface.
The optional
.Cm transport
setting accepts
.Cm legacy
or
.Cm modern .
If omitted, the legacy PCI transport and device identity are retained for
compatibility.
The modern setting selects the non-transitional VirtIO PCI transport and
device ID 0x1049.
For modern devices,
.Cm packed=true
enables the packed virtqueue and is disabled by default.
It supports selective virtqueue reset without discarding the active 9P
session or its fid namespace.
.It Cm virtio-fs
Modern-only, non-DAX Virtio filesystem interface using non-transitional
device ID 0x105a.
The required
.Cm path
setting is the absolute pathname of a local
.Dv SOCK_SEQPACKET
backend socket speaking the versioned VFSB protocol.
The required
.Cm tag
setting is the UTF-8 mount tag exposed to the guest and is limited to 36
bytes.
The optional
.Cm queues
setting accepts 1 through 64 request queues and defaults to one; the device
also exposes one high-priority queue.
The optional
.Cm packed=true
setting enables packed virtqueues and is disabled by default.
The optional
.Cm identity
setting supplies a stable backend identity for checkpoint compatibility.
When it is present,
.Nm
requires the backend to negotiate explicit freeze and state-transfer support.
Checkpoint then uses bounded, event-driven QUIESCE and THAW exchanges; the
backend returns an opaque state record when quiescing and consumes that record
when thawing.
The identity and negotiated backend limits must match at restore.
If
.Cm identity
is omitted, ordinary filesystem operation remains available but checkpointing
this device returns
.Er ENOTSUP .
The backend peer must have the same effective user and group identity as
.Nm
and must negotiate cancellation support.
The device therefore advertises selective virtqueue reset.
It does not advertise notifications, DAX, or a shared-memory region.
The tree currently provides the device-side protocol and test peers, not a
production filesystem backend daemon.
.It Cm virtio-rnd
Virtio RNG interface.
The optional
.Cm transport
setting accepts
.Cm legacy
or
.Cm modern .
If omitted, the legacy PCI transport and its existing device identity are used
for compatibility with existing guests and launch scripts.
The modern setting selects the non-transitional VirtIO PCI transport and
device ID 0x1044.
For qualification of packed virtqueues, the experimental
.Cm packed=true
setting advertises
.Dv VIRTIO_F_RING_PACKED .
It is valid only with
.Cm transport=modern
and is disabled by default.
.It Cm virtio-pmem
Virtio persistent-memory interface.
This device is modern PCI only, uses device ID 27 and non-transitional PCI
device ID 0x105b, and exposes its bytes through shared-memory region zero.
The required
.Cm path
setting names a nonempty regular backing file.
.Nm
maps that file shared, holds a nonblocking exclusive advisory lock, and
rejects symbolic links, competing device instances, and backing identity or
size changes detected at a flush boundary.
The required
.Cm id
setting supplies a stable operator identity of at most 255 bytes.
It is the portable external-backend contract: restore requires the same
identity and size, while host paths, inode numbers, mappings, and file
descriptors are deliberately not serialized.
Operators must assign a separate backing file to each device unless the
workload explicitly accepts the sharing risks described by VirtIO 1.4.
The device completes a guest FLUSH only after synchronous mapping and file
persistence; a backend failure is returned as the specification-defined
minus one.
Reset, selective queue reset, suspend, and checkpoint close admission and use
a bounded event-driven drain.
Checkpoint adds one final persistence operation before publishing portable
state, and a failed checkpoint reopens the still-running source
transactionally.
The optional
.Cm packed=true
setting advertises packed virtqueues and is disabled by default.
.It Cm virtio-gpu
Virtio GPU 2D interface.
This device is modern PCI only and uses non-transitional device ID 0x1050.
It exposes one scanout, a control queue, and a cursor queue.
The optional
.Cm width
and
.Cm height
settings select the scanout dimensions and default to 1024 by 768.
The selected pair must fit the EDID 1.4 detailed-timing representation.
The initial feature surface supports the eight baseline 32-bit 2D formats,
resource creation and backing, guest-to-host transfers, scanout selection,
flush, the 64 by 64 cursor protocol, and
.Dv VIRTIO_GPU_F_EDID .
The EDID response is a deterministic one-block EDID 1.4 description of the
configured virtual monitor.
The device does not advertise accelerated rendering, resource UUIDs, or
context initialization.
The experimental
.Cm blob=true
setting advertises guest-memory blob resources and blob alignment, and adds a
256 MiB sparse host-visible shared-memory aperture in a dedicated 64-bit BAR.
It accepts guest blobs, including Linux's shareable dumb-buffer convention,
but not host-renderer or cross-device blobs.
Mapped aperture accesses are reported uncached and are translated through the
same architecture-neutral DMA boundary as descriptor-backed resources.
The setting is disabled by default pending live Linux and checkpoint
qualification.
If no host display consumer is configured, the scanout remains headless while
the complete guest-visible resource and queue behavior remains active.
The optional
.Cm display=true
setting registers the Virtio GPU scanout and cursor as the process-wide
framebuffer producer.
To expose that producer over VNC, configure one
.Cm fbuf
device with
.Cm source=external,vga=off
and with
.Cm w
and
.Cm h
equal to the Virtio GPU dimensions.
Framebuffer producer ownership is exclusive; configuration fails instead of
silently replacing another display device.
The optional
.Cm packed=true
setting advertises
.Dv VIRTIO_F_RING_PACKED
and is disabled by default.
Checkpoint state uses a bounded, versioned little-endian representation of
monitor identity, resources, host pixels, guest physical backing descriptors,
blob identities and mappings, scanout, and cursor state.
Restore rejects a destination configured with different monitor dimensions.
Host mappings, pointers, display handles, and architecture-specific state are
never serialized.
.It Cm virtio-mem
Virtio memory hotplug interface.
This device is modern PCI only and uses the non-transitional device ID
0x1058.
The required
.Cm size
setting reserves a device-memory region outside ordinary boot RAM.
The optional
.Cm requested
setting asks the guest to plug that amount initially and defaults to zero.
The optional
.Cm block-size
setting defaults to 2 MiB.
The region and requested sizes must be block aligned, the block size must be a
power of two and at least one host page, and the requested size cannot exceed
the region size.
The optional
.Cm packed=true
setting advertises
.Dv VIRTIO_F_RING_PACKED
and is disabled by default.
The device does not advertise ACPI proximity domains,
.Dv VIRTIO_MEM_F_UNPLUGGED_INACCESSIBLE ,
or persistent suspend.
Consequently, unplugged blocks remain CPU-addressable but their contents are
undefined and their host pages may be reclaimed.
Ordinary VirtIO reset preserves plugged blocks as required by the
specification.
Checkpoint state uses a bounded, versioned little-endian bitmap and a generic
device-memory payload record; host mappings, pointers, file descriptors, and
architecture-specific state are not serialized.
.It Cm virtio-snd
Virtio sound interface.
This device is modern PCI only and uses the non-transitional device ID
0x1059.
It exposes the standard control, event, playback, and capture queues, with
one signed 16-bit mono or stereo output stream and one input stream at
44.1 or 48 kHz.
The current deterministic null backend discards output and returns zero-filled
input; it is intended for guest-driver, queue, lifecycle, and checkpoint
qualification and does not connect to host audio hardware.
The optional
.Cm backend=null
setting selects this behavior.
The provisional
.Cm backend=oss
setting uses nonblocking OSS playback and capture devices.
The
.Cm play=/dev/dsp
and
.Cm record=/dev/dsp
settings select those endpoints.
OSS readiness is integrated with the event loop; a vCPU notification never
waits for host audio I/O, and at most one bounded request is retained per
stream.
The OSS mode remains provisional pending live Linux playback, capture, reset,
and checkpoint qualification with representative host audio devices.
The optional
.Cm packed=true
setting advertises
.Dv VIRTIO_F_RING_PACKED
and is disabled by default.
Checkpoint state is fixed-width, versioned, little-endian, and contains no
host pointers, file descriptors, locks, or architecture-specific state.
It records the backend kind and exact configured playback and capture paths;
restore rejects a destination configured with different audio side effects.
.It Cm virtio-iommu
Modern-only VirtIO IOMMU interface.
This option enables a PCI IOMMU passthrough binding model for modern virtio
endpoints and supports non-shared request/event queues.
The optional
.Cm packed=true
setting advertises
.Dv VIRTIO_F_RING_PACKED
for qualification.
.It Cm virtio-balloon
Virtio traditional memory-balloon interface.
This device is modern PCI only and uses the non-transitional device ID
0x1045.
The optional
.Cm target
setting selects the startup balloon target as a memory size.
The target must be a multiple of 4096 bytes and cannot exceed configured
guest RAM.
The guest reports its current balloon size through the standard
.Dv actual
configuration field.
The optional
.Cm stats_interval=N
setting advertises
.Dv VIRTIO_BALLOON_F_STATS_VQ
and requests refreshed guest memory statistics every
.Ar N
seconds, where
.Ar N
is between 1 and 3600.
The device retains at most one statistics buffer and uses a monotonic,
event-driven timer.
Omitting the setting leaves the optional queue unadvertised.
The optional
.Cm deflate_on_oom=true
setting advertises
.Dv VIRTIO_BALLOON_F_DEFLATE_ON_OOM ,
allowing a conforming guest to return ballooned pages through the deflate
queue when required for guest stability even when the configured target has
not decreased.
The option changes only guest policy permission: returned pages still use the
normal validated deflate path and, because
.Dv VIRTIO_BALLOON_F_MUST_TELL_HOST
is also offered, cannot be reused until the device acknowledges them.
It is disabled by default.
The optional
.Cm free_page_hinting=true
setting advertises
.Dv VIRTIO_BALLOON_F_FREE_PAGE_HINT
and exposes the standard free-page-hint virtqueue.
The device accepts the standard command identifier before processing writable
guest-RAM ranges, bounds each notification by the queue size, and stops the
current round if a range cannot be reverse-mapped or discarded.
Hinting is disabled by default pending live Linux qualification.
The optional
.Cm free_page_reporting=true
setting advertises
.Dv VIRTIO_BALLOON_F_PAGE_REPORTING
and exposes the standard reporting virtqueue.
Each request must contain only device-writable guest-RAM ranges.
For each native-page-aligned range,
.Nm
may release the corresponding host backing before acknowledging the request;
unmappable or unaligned ranges are safely ignored because page release is an
optional optimization.
Reporting is disabled by default pending live Linux qualification.
The optional
.Cm page_poison=true
setting advertises
.Dv VIRTIO_BALLOON_F_PAGE_POISON .
The guest may write the standard
.Dv poison_val
configuration field before setting
.Dv DRIVER_OK ;
attempts to change it while the device is operational are ignored.
When page poisoning and free-page reporting are both negotiated,
.Nm
preserves the reported guest-memory contents instead of applying the optional
host discard optimization, as required to retain the poison pattern.
Page poisoning is disabled by default.
The optional
.Cm packed=true
setting advertises
.Dv VIRTIO_F_RING_PACKED
for qualification and is disabled by default.
Balloon page frame numbers always describe 4096-byte units.
.Nm
advertises
.Dv VIRTIO_BALLOON_F_MUST_TELL_HOST
because its host discard operation may be deferred.
On deflation, cancellation of the discard hint is requested for the complete
native host page before the request is acknowledged.
Host discard and cancellation calls are paging optimizations: their failure is
traced but does not reverse the standard ownership transfer represented by a
completed request, which has no status field.
On hosts with a larger native page size,
.Nm
waits until every 4096-byte unit in a native page has been inflated before
reclaiming that native page.
Invalid or non-RAM page frame numbers are ignored without preventing valid
entries in the same request from completing.
Checkpoint state preserves the target and guest-reported actual size, but does
not serialize host pointers or a reconstructed page-frame list.
.It Cm virtio-rtc
Virtio RTC interface.
This device is modern PCI only and uses the non-transitional device ID
0x1051.
It exposes one dense UTC-like clock through the standard request queue.
Clock readings are unsigned little-endian nanoseconds since the Unix epoch
and are obtained after the request becomes available.
The clock type is
.Dv VIRTIO_RTC_CLOCK_UTC_MAYBE_SMEARED
because host
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME
policy may either step or smear around a leap second.
By default, the feature surface does not advertise alarms or cross timestamps;
alarm requests then return
.Dv VIRTIO_RTC_S_ENODEV ,
and unsupported cross-timestamp reads return
.Dv VIRTIO_RTC_S_EOPNOTSUPP .
The optional
.Cm alarm=true
setting advertises
.Dv VIRTIO_RTC_F_ALARM
and adds the standard alarm notification queue.
Alarm control uses nanoseconds on clock zero, and expiration delivery is
driven by a host timer rather than periodic polling.
The optional
.Cm packed=true
setting advertises
.Dv VIRTIO_F_RING_PACKED
for qualification and is disabled by default.
Checkpoint state is a fixed-width version record containing only portable
alarm state; host clock values, timer handles, and host pointers are never
serialized.
.It Cm virtio-console
Virtio console interface, which exposes multiple ports
to the guest in the form of simple char devices for simple IO
between the guest and host userspaces.
The optional
.Cm transport
setting accepts
.Cm legacy
or
.Cm modern .
If omitted, the legacy PCI transport and device identity are retained for
compatibility.
The modern setting selects the non-transitional VirtIO PCI transport and
device ID 0x1043.
For modern devices,
.Cm packed=true
enables packed virtqueues and is disabled by default.
Each configured port accepts a
.Cm console
boolean, disabled by default, which nominates that port to the guest as a
text console after the guest acknowledges the port.
More than one port may be nominated.
The legacy
.Fl s
option syntax spells the same selection as
.Cm console-port= Ns Ar N
and may be repeated.
.It Cm virtio-input
Virtio input interface.
The required
.Cm path
setting names a host evdev device, such as
.Pa /dev/input/event0 .
.Nm
opens the device for reading and writing and obtains an exclusive evdev grab.
The path may refer to physical hardware or to a virtual device created by a
long-running host service through
.Pa /dev/uinput .
The provider must create the evdev device before
.Nm
starts and keep it alive while the virtual machine is running.
The optional
.Cm transport
setting accepts
.Cm legacy
or
.Cm modern .
If omitted, the standards-compliant non-transitional VirtIO PCI transport and
device ID 0x1052 are used.
The explicit
.Cm legacy
setting retains bhyve's historical register interface for compatibility, but
VirtIO 1.4 assigns no transitional PCI device ID to the input device.
With the modern transport,
.Cm packed=true
enables packed virtqueues and is disabled by default.
.It Cm virtio-vsock
Virtio vsock interface for host-guest socket transport.
The device is configured with
.Cm cid
(guest context identifier, from 3 through 4294967294).
The optional
.Cm backend
setting accepts
.Cm userspace
or
.Cm kernel
and defaults to
.Cm userspace .
The userspace backend also requires
.Cm path
(existing host directory for Unix domain sockets).
The optional
.Cm transport
setting accepts
.Cm legacy
or
.Cm modern .
If omitted, the legacy PCI transport is used for compatibility with existing
guests and launch scripts.
The modern setting selects the non-transitional VirtIO PCI transport and
device ID 0x1053.
For modern devices,
.Cm packed=true
enables packed virtqueues and is disabled by default.
Guest connections to a host port use a socket named for the port inside this
directory.
For host-to-guest connections, host processes connect to
.Pa <path>/sock
and send a 16-byte control message containing the command, guest port, socket
type, and status fields.
The kernel backend does not accept
.Cm path .
It attaches the VM to the host
.Dv AF_VSOCK
domain through
.Pa /dev/vsock ,
so host applications use ordinary
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr bind 2 ,
and
.Xr connect 2
calls with local CID 2 and the configured guest CID.
Kernel backends for distinct guest CIDs may run concurrently.
A second instance using an already attached CID fails with
.Er EADDRINUSE .
The host routes each connection and provider reset by its remote guest CID.
.Pp
Checkpoint state for either backend is portable only while no vsock
connection or buffered provider packet is active.
The kernel backend uses the
.Xr vsock 4
provider freeze/thaw ioctls to enforce that condition atomically.
The userspace backend rejects a checkpoint while Unix relay or control
connections remain because their host descriptors cannot be reconstructed
in another process.
See
.Xr vsock 4
for details.
.Pp
Every
.Cm modern
VirtIO device listed above advertises the VirtIO 1.4
.Dv VIRTIO_F_RING_RESET
facility.
A guest may reset one virtqueue without resetting the device.
While a queue reset is in progress,
.Nm
rejects notifications and configuration changes for that queue; after the
reset completes, the guest may configure new queue addresses, size, and
interrupt vector before enabling it again.
The
.Cm legacy
transport does not advertise this facility.
.It Cm ahci
AHCI controller attached to arbitrary devices.
.It Cm ahci-cd
AHCI controller attached to an ATAPI CD/DVD.
.It Cm ahci-hd
AHCI controller attached to a SATA hard drive.
.It Cm e1000
Intel e82545 network interface.
.It Cm uart
PCI 16550 serial device.
.It Cm lpc
LPC PCI-ISA bridge with COM1, COM2, COM3, and COM4 16550 serial ports,
a boot ROM, and,
optionally, a TPM module, a fw_cfg type, and the debug/test device.
The LPC bridge emulation can only be configured on bus 0.
.It Cm fbuf
Raw framebuffer device attached to VNC server.
.It Cm xhci
eXtensible Host Controller Interface (xHCI) USB controller.
.It Cm nvme
NVM Express (NVMe) controller.
.It Cm hda
High Definition Audio Controller.
.El
.Pp
The optional parameter
.Ar conf
describes the backend for device emulations.
If
.Ar conf
is not specified, the device emulation has no backend and can be
considered unconnected.
.Ss Network device backends
.Sm off
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Xo
.Cm tap Ar N
.Op Cm \&,mac= Ar xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx
.Op Cm \&,mtu= Ar N
.Op Cm \&,queues= Ar N
.Xc
.It
.Xo
.Cm vmnet Ar N
.Op Cm \&,mac= Ar xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx
.Op Cm \&,mtu= Ar N
.Op Cm \&,queues= Ar N
.Xc
.It
.Cm ngd Ar N
.It
.Xo
.Cm netgraph,path= Ar ADDRESS Cm \&,peerhook= Ar HOOK
.Op Cm \&,socket= Ar NAME
.Op Cm \&,hook= Ar HOOK
.Op Cm \&,mac= Ar xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx
.Op Cm \&,mtu= Ar N
.Op Cm \&,queues= Ar N
.Xc
.It
.Xo
.Cm slirp
.Op Cm \&,open
.Op Cm \&,hostfwd= Ar proto : Ar hostaddr : Ar hostport - Ar guestaddr : Ar guestport
.Op Cm \&,mac= Ar xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx
.Op Cm \&,mtu= Ar N
.Op Cm \&,queues= Ar N
.Xc
.El
.Sm on
.Pp
If
.Cm mac
is not specified, the MAC address is derived from a fixed OUI, and the
remaining bytes from an MD5 hash of the slot and function numbers and
the device name.
If specified, it must be a unicast MAC address.
.Pp
The MAC address is an ASCII string in
.Xr ethers 5
format.
.Pp
A
.Cm ngd
device can be used to connect a guest to a
.Xr netgraph 4
through a
.Xr ng_device 4
node.
This can be used to run bhyve in a
.Xr VNET 9
jail, and give it access to the host's netgraph, that cannot be reached
directly, by exposing the ng_device through
.Xr devfs 8 .
.Pp
With
.Cm virtio-net
devices, the
.Cm mtu
parameter can be specified to inform the guest about the largest MTU
that should be allowed, expressed in bytes.
.Pp
The
.Cm queues
parameter configures between 1 and 8 transmit/receive queue pairs for a
.Cm virtio-net
device.
The default is 1.
Values greater than 1 require
.Cm transport=modern
and advertise the VirtIO control-queue, automatic multiqueue steering, and
receive-side scaling and receive-hash-reporting features.
Only the first queue pair is active after reset; a conforming guest enables
additional pairs with a VirtIO network multiqueue control command.
RSS-capable guests can program a 128-entry indirection table, a 40-byte
Toeplitz key, and IPv4, IPv6, TCP, and UDP hash types.
The device writes the resulting hash value and protocol report into the
extended receive header while retaining the base header expected by host
network backends.
.Pp
The optional
.Cm checkpoint_identity
parameter supplies an opaque, stable network-backend identity for checkpoint
restore and migration.
It must be nonempty and at most 255 bytes, and must identify the same logical
network attachment at the destination.
When omitted, the backend type and configured backend name form a host-local
identity; an explicit value is recommended for cross-host migration.
.Pp
With
.Cm netgraph
backend, the
.Cm path
and
.Cm peerhook
parameters must be specified to set the destination node and corresponding hook.
The optional parameters
.Cm socket
and
.Cm hook
may be used to set the
.Xr ng_socket 4
node name and source hook.
The
.Ar ADDRESS ,
.Ar HOOK ,
and
.Ar NAME
must comply with
.Xr netgraph 4
addressing rules.
.Pp
The
.Cm slirp
backend can be used to provide a NATed network to the guest.
This backend has limited performance but does not require any network
configuration on the host system and can be used by unprivileged users.
It depends on the
.Pa net/libslirp
port.
If the
.Cm open
keyword is set, the guest will be able to make outbound network
connections, and
.Nm
will transparently handle the necessary address translation.
The
.Cm hostfwd
option takes a 5-tuple describing how connections from the host are to be
forwarded to the guest.
Multiple rules can be specified, separated by semicolons.
Note that semicolons must be escaped or quoted to prevent the shell from
interpreting them.
The backend will provide DHCP and DNS service to the guest.
.Ss Block storage device backends:
.Bl -bullet
.Sm off
.It
.Ar /filename Op Cm \&, Ar block-device-options
.It
.Ar /dev/xxx Op Cm \&, Ar block-device-options
.Sm on
.El
.Pp
The
.Ar block-device-options
are:
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Cm nocache
Open the file with
.Dv O_DIRECT .
.It Cm direct
Open the file using
.Dv O_SYNC .
.It Cm ro
Force the file to be opened read-only.
.It Cm sectorsize= Ns Ar logical Ns Oo Cm \&/ Ns Ar physical Oc
Specify the logical and physical sector sizes of the emulated disk.
The physical sector size is optional and is equal to the logical sector size
if not explicitly specified.
.It Cm nodelete
Disable emulation of guest trim requests via
.Dv DIOCGDELETE
requests.
.It Cm checkpoint_identity= Ns Ar identifier
Set the stable identity used to verify that checkpoint restore or migration
has opened the intended backing store.
The identifier must be nonempty and at most 255 bytes.
It is an opaque operator contract and must name the same logical volume on
the source and destination.
When omitted,
.Nm
derives a host-local identity from the already-open file or device.
That fallback detects replacement of a path on the same host, but is
deliberately not portable across hosts; cross-host migration should always
specify an explicit identity.
.It Li bootindex= Ns Ar index
Add the device to the boot order at
.Ar index .
A fw_cfg file is used to specify the boot order.
The guest firmware may ignore or doesn't support this fw_cfg file.
In that case, this feature doesn't work as expected.
.El
.Ss SCSI device backends
.Bl -bullet
.Sm off
.It
.Pa /dev/cam/ctl Oo Ar pp Cm \&. Ar vp Oc Oo Cm \&, Ar scsi-device-options Oc
.Sm on
.El
.Pp
The
.Ar scsi-device-options
are:
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Cm iid= Ns Ar IID
Initiator ID to use when sending requests to specified CTL port.
The default value is 0.
.It Li bootindex= Ns Ar index
Add the device to the boot order at
.Ar index .
A fw_cfg file is used to specify the boot order.
The guest firmware may ignore or not support this fw_cfg file.
In that case, this feature doesn't work as expected.
.El
.Ss 9P device backends
.Bl -bullet
.Sm off
.It
.Ar sharename Cm = Ar /path/to/share Op Cm \&, Ar 9p-device-options
.Sm on
.El
.Pp
The
.Ar 9p-device-options
are:
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Cm ro
Expose the share in read-only mode.
.It Cm transport= Ns Ar transport
Select
.Cm legacy
or
.Cm modern
VirtIO PCI transport.
The default is
.Cm legacy .
.El
.Ss TTY device backends
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Cm stdio
Connect the serial port to the standard input and output of
the
.Nm
process.
.It Ar /dev/xxx
Use the host TTY device for serial port I/O.
.It Ar tcp=ip:port
Use the TCP server for serial port I/O.
Configuring this option will start a TCP server that waits for connections.
Only one connection is allowed at any time.
The TCP server will immediately close new connections while an existing
connection is active.
Note that this feature allows unprivileged users to access the guest console,
so ensure that access is appropriately restricted.
.El
.Pp
Any TTY backend may be followed by a
.Cm \&, Ns Cm log= Ns Ar path
suffix, for example
.Ar tcp=127.0.0.1:4321,log=/var/log/vm0-console.log
or
.Cm stdio Ns Cm ,log= Ns Ar path .
Every byte the guest transmits on the serial port is appended to
.Ar path
unconditionally, independent of the backend and of whether any client is
attached.
Unlike the plain
.Ar tcp=
backend, which drops output while no client is connected, the log file
captures the entire console stream, including boot output emitted before a
client connects.
This makes it suitable for capturing headless or automated boots.
.Ss TPM device backends
.Bl -bullet
.Sm off
.It
.Ar type Ns \&, Ns Ar path Ns Op Cm \&, Ns Ar tpm-device-options
.Sm on
.El
.Pp
Emulate a TPM device.
Supported options for
.Ar type :
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Cm passthru
Use a physical TPM device.
The argument
.Ar path
needs to point to a valid TPM device path, i.e.
.Pa /dev/tpm0 .
.It Cm swtpm
Connect to a running
.Cm swtpm
instance.
The argument
.Ar path
needs to point to a UNIX domain socket that a
.Cm swtpm
process is listening on.
.El
.Pp
The
.Ar tpm-device-options
are:
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Cm version= Ns Ar version
Version of the TPM device according to the TCG specification.
Defaults to
.Cm 2.0 ,
which is the only version currently supported.
.El
.Ss Boot ROM device backends
.Sm off
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Ar romfile Ns Op Cm \&, Ns Ar varfile
.El
.Sm on
.Pp
Map
.Ar romfile
in the guest address space reserved for boot firmware.
.Pp
If
.Ar varfile
is provided, that file is also mapped in the boot firmware guest
address space, and any modifications the guest makes will be saved
to that file.
.Pp
fw_cfg types:
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Ar fwcfg
The fw_cfg interface is used to pass information such as the CPU count
or ACPI tables to the guest firmware.
Supported values are
.Ql bhyve
and
.Ql qemu .
Due to backward compatibility reasons,
.Ql bhyve
is the default option.
When
.Ql bhyve
is used, bhyve's fwctl interface is used.
It currently reports only the CPU count to the guest firmware.
The
.Ql qemu
option uses QEMU's fw_cfg interface.
This interface is widely used and allows user-defined information to
be passed to the guest.
It is used for passing the CPU count, ACPI tables, a boot order and
many other things to the guest.
Some operating systems such as Fedora CoreOS can be configured by
QEMU's fw_cfg interface as well.
.El
.Ss Pass-through device backends
.Sm off
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Cm ppt Ar N Oo , Ar passthru-device-options Oc
.It
.Ns Ar bus Cm \&/ Ar slot Cm \&/ Ar function
.Op , Ar passthru-device-options
.It
.Cm pci Ar bus Cm : Ar slot Cm : Ns Ar function
.Op , Ar passthru-device-options
.El
.Sm on
.Pp
Connect to a PCI device on the host either named ppt
.Ns Ar N
or at the selector described by
.Ar slot ,
.Ar bus ,
and
.Ar function
numbers.
.Pp
The
.Ar passthru-device-options
are:
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Cm rom= Ns Ar romfile
Add
.Ar romfile
as option ROM to the PCI device.
The ROM will be loaded by firmware and should be capable of
initializing the device.
.It Li bootindex= Ns Ar index
Add the device to the boot order at
.Ar index .
A fw_cfg file is used to specify the boot order.
The guest firmware may ignore or doesn't support this fw_cfg file.
In that case, this feature doesn't work as expected.
.El
.Pp
Guest memory must be wired using the
.Fl S
option when a pass-through device is configured.
.Pp
The host device must have been reserved at boot time using the
.Va pptdevs
loader variable as described in
.Xr vmm 4 .
.Ss Virtio console device backends
.Bl -bullet
.Sm off
.It
.Cm port1= Ns Ar /path/to/port1.sock Ns Op Cm ,port Ns Ar N Cm \&= Ns Ar /path/to/port2.sock No \~ Ar ...
.Sm on
.El
.Pp
A maximum of 16 ports per device can be created.
Every port is named and corresponds to a Unix domain socket created by
.Nm .
.Nm
accepts at most one connection per port at a time.
.Pp
Limitations:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Due to the lack of destructors in
.Nm ,
sockets on the filesystem must be cleaned up manually after
.Nm
exits.
.It
There is no way to use the
.Dq console port
feature, nor the console port
resize at present.
.It
Emergency write is advertised, but no-op at present.
.El
.Ss Virtio input device backends:
.Bl -bullet
.Sm off
.It
.Ar /dev/input/eventX
.Sm on
.El
.Pp
Send input events of
.Ar /dev/input/eventX
to guest by VirtIO Input Interface.
The event device can be physical hardware or a virtual device created by a
host service through
.Pa /dev/uinput .
Such a service must create the event device before
.Nm
starts and keep it alive until
.Nm
exits.
.Ss Framebuffer device backends
.Bl -bullet
.Sm off
.It
.Op Cm rfb= Ar address
.Op Cm ,w= Ar width
.Op Cm ,h= Ar height
.Op Cm ,vga= Ar vgaconf
.Op Cm ,source= Ar source
.Op Cm ,wait
.Op Cm ,password= Ar password
.Sm on
.El
.Pp
Configuration options are defined as follows:
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Cm rfb= Ns Ar address Pq or Cm tcp= Ns Ar address
A UNIX domain socket or IP address and a port VNC should listen on.
There are three possible formats:
.Pp
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
.Sm off
.Op Ar IPv4 Cm \&:
.Ar port
.Sm on
.It
.Sm off
.Cm \&[ Ar IPv6%zone Cm \&] Cm \&: Ar port
.Sm on
.It
.Sm off
.Cm unix: Ar my/unix.sock
.Sm on
.El
.Pp
The default is to listen on localhost IPv4 address and default VNC port 5900.
An IPv6 address must be enclosed in square brackets and may contain an
optional zone identifier.
.It Cm w= Ns Ar width No and Cm h= Ns Ar height
A display resolution, width and height, respectively.
If not specified, a default resolution of 1024x768 pixels will be used.
Minimal supported resolution is 640x480 pixels,
and maximum is 3840x2160 pixels.
.It Cm vga= Ns Ar vgaconf
Possible values for this option are
.Cm io
(default),
.Cm on ,
and
.Cm off .
PCI graphics cards have a dual personality in that they are
standard PCI devices with BAR addressing, but may also
implicitly decode legacy VGA I/O space
.Pq Ad 0x3c0-3df
and memory space
.Pq 64 KiB at Ad 0xA0000 .
The default
.Cm io
option should be used for guests that attempt to issue BIOS calls which result
in I/O port queries, and fail to boot if I/O decode is disabled.
.Pp
The
.Cm on
option should be used along with the CSM BIOS capability in UEFI
to boot traditional BIOS guests that require the legacy VGA I/O and
memory regions to be available.
.Pp
The
.Cm off
option should be used for the UEFI guests that assume that
VGA adapter is present if they detect the I/O ports.
An example of such a guest is
.Ox
in UEFI mode.
.It Cm source= Ns Ar source
Selects the framebuffer producer.
The default,
.Cm fbuf ,
uses the emulated VGA framebuffer.
.Cm external
leaves pixel production to a separately configured display device, such as
.Cm virtio-gpu,display=true ,
while retaining this device's VNC server and framebuffer storage.
The external setting requires
.Cm vga=off .
Only one producer may own the framebuffer, and its dimensions must match
.Cm w
and
.Cm h .
.Pp
Please refer to the
.Nm
.Fx
wiki page
.Pq Lk https://wiki.freebsd.org/bhyve
for configuration notes of particular guests.
.It Cm wait
Instruct
.Nm
to only boot upon the initiation of a VNC connection, simplifying the
installation of operating systems that require immediate keyboard input.
This can be removed for post-installation use.
.It Cm password= Ns Ar password
This type of authentication is known to be cryptographically weak and is not
intended for use on untrusted networks.
Many implementations will want to use stronger security, such as running
the session over an encrypted channel provided by IPsec or SSH.
.El
.Ss xHCI USB device backends
.Bl -bullet
.Sm off
.It
.Ar tablet
.Sm on
.El
.Pp
A USB tablet device that provides precise cursor synchronization
when using VNC.
.Ss NVMe device backends
.Bl -bullet
.Sm off
.It
.Ar devpath
.Op Cm ,maxq= Ar #
.Op Cm ,qsz= Ar #
.Op Cm ,ioslots= Ar #
.Op Cm ,sectsz= Ar #
.Op Cm ,ser= Ar #
.Op Cm ,eui64= Ar #
.Op Cm ,dsm= Ar opt
.Sm on
.El
.Pp
Configuration options are defined as follows:
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Ar devpath
Accepted device paths are:
.Ar /dev/blockdev
or
.Ar /path/to/image
or
.Cm ram= Ns Ar size_in_MiB .
.It Cm maxq
Max number of queues.
.It Cm qsz
Max elements in each queue.
.It Cm ioslots
Max number of concurrent I/O requests.
.It Cm sectsz
Sector size (defaults to blockif sector size).
.It Cm ser
Serial number with maximum 20 characters.
.It Cm eui64
IEEE Extended Unique Identifier (8 byte value).
.It Cm dsm
Dataset Management support.
Supported values are:
.Cm auto , enable ,
and
.Cm disable .
.El
.Ss AHCI device backends
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sm off
.Op Oo Cm hd\&: | cd\&: Oc Ar path
.Op Cm ,nmrr= Ar nmrr
.Op Cm ,ser= Ar #
.Op Cm ,rev= Ar #
.Op Cm ,model= Ar #
.Sm on
.El
.Pp
Configuration options are defined as follows:
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Cm nmrr
Nominal Media Rotation Rate, known as RPM.
A value of 1 indicates that the device is a solid state drive, i.e.,
non-rotational.
Default value is 0.
.It Cm ser
Serial number with maximum 20 characters.
.It Cm rev
Revision number with maximum 8 characters.
.It Cm model
Model number with maximum 40 characters.
.El
.Ss HD Audio device backends
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sm off
.Op Cm play= Ar playback
.Op Cm ,rec= Ar recording
.Sm on
.El
.Pp
Configuration options are defined as follows:
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Cm play
Playback device, typically
.Ar /dev/dsp0 .
.It Cm rec
Recording device, typically
.Ar /dev/dsp0 .
.El
.Sh CONFIGURATION VARIABLES
.Nm
uses an internal tree of configuration variables to describe global and
per-device settings.
When
.Nm
starts,
it parses command line options (including config files) in the order given
on the command line.
Each command line option sets one or more configuration variables.
For example,
the
.Fl s
option creates a new tree node for a PCI device and sets one or more variables
under that node including the device model and device model-specific variables.
Variables may be set multiple times during this parsing stage with the final
value overriding previous values.
.Pp
Once all of the command line options have been processed,
the configuration values are frozen.
.Nm
then uses the value of configuration values to initialize device models
and global settings.
.Pp
More details on configuration variables can be found in
.Xr bhyve_config 5 .
.Sh CONFIGURATION FILE CREATION
The
.Fl k
flag allows one to provide a path to a configuration file holding all
settings, which otherwise would need to be defined by providing a long
list of program arguments to
.Nm .
.Pp
There is a very simple way to translate a complex set of program
arguments to an equivalent configuration file in
.Xr bhyve_config 5
format.
.Pp
Use
.Fl o
.Ar config.dump=1
to make
.Nm
dump a configuration file representing the used flags and arguments to
stdout. You can pipe the output into a file to persist the generated settings.
.Pp
Make sure to remove the
.Ar config.dump
line from the resulting configuration file before using it to start
.Nm .
.Sh DEBUG SERVER
The current debug server provides limited support for debuggers.
.Ss Registers
Each virtual CPU is exposed to the debugger as a thread.
.Pp
General purpose registers can be queried for each virtual CPU, but other
registers such as floating-point and system registers cannot be queried.
.Ss Memory
Memory (including memory mapped I/O regions) can be read and written
by the debugger.
Memory operations use virtual addresses that are resolved to physical
addresses via the current virtual CPU's active address translation.
.Ss Control
The running guest can be interrupted by the debugger at any time
.Pq for example, by pressing Ctrl-C in the debugger .
.Pp
Single stepping is only supported on Intel CPUs supporting the MTRAP VM exit.
.Pp
Breakpoints are supported on Intel CPUs that support single stepping.
Note that continuing from a breakpoint while interrupts are enabled in the
guest may not work as expected due to timer interrupts firing while single
stepping over the breakpoint.
.Sh VIRTIO DIAGNOSTICS
When built with DTrace support,
.Nm
provides
.Dq virtio
USDT probes for feature and status negotiation, queue enable, notification,
accepted direct and indirect descriptor chains, split and packed event-index
decisions, queue-reset begin, completion and failure, configuration-window
access, configuration changes, network receive hash-report publication,
device reset, and transport errors.
The first argument of every probe is the device-model name, such as
.Dq vtnet ,
.Dq vtblk ,
or
.Dq vtvsock ,
so events from devices with the same queue index can be distinguished.
They can be listed with:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dtrace -l -P virtio
.Ed
.Pp
Queue-reset probes include the queue index and a generation number, allowing a
consumer to correlate an asynchronous reset completion with the reset that
started it.
The failure probe also includes the backend error.
.Pp
The descriptor-chain probe reports the queue, ring layout, whether the request
used an indirect table, and the number of data descriptors accepted by the
common decoder.
The two event-index probes report the driver threshold, the device producer
cursor, and the resulting interrupt decision for split and packed rings.
The
.Dq net-rx-hash
probe reports the receive virtqueue, hash value, specification-defined packet
class, and frame length when
.Dv VIRTIO_NET_F_HASH_REPORT
metadata is published to a guest.
These probes are dormant unless a DTrace consumer enables them and therefore
do not add diagnostic logging to the queue hot path.
For example, the following reports queue-reset failures from running
.Nm
processes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dtrace -n 'virtio*:::transport-queue-reset-fail {
	printf("%s queue=%u generation=%u error=%d\en",
	    copyinstr(arg0), arg1, arg2, arg3);
}'
.Ed
.Sh SIGNAL HANDLING
.Nm
deals with the following signals:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width SIGTERM -compact
.It SIGTERM
Trigger ACPI poweroff for a VM
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
Exit status indicates how the VM was terminated:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width indent -compact
.It 0
rebooted
.It 1
powered off
.It 2
halted
.It 3
triple fault (amd64 only)
.It 4
exited due to an error
.It 5
suspended
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
If not using a boot ROM, the guest operating system must have been loaded with
.Xr bhyveload 8
or a similar boot loader before
.Xr bhyve 4
can be run.
Otherwise, the boot loader is not needed.
.Pp
To run a virtual machine with 1 GiB of memory, two virtual CPUs, a virtio
block device backed by the
.Pa /my/image
filesystem image, and a serial port for the console:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bhyve -c 2 -s 0,hostbridge -s 1,lpc -s 2,virtio-blk,/my/image \\
  -l com1,stdio -H -P -m 1G vm1
.Ed
.Pp
To do the same on arm64:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bhyve -c 2 -s 0,hostbridge -s 1,virtio-blk,/my/image -o console=stdio \\
  -o bootrom=/usr/local/share/u-boot/u-boot-bhyve-arm64/u-boot.bin -m 1G vm1
.Ed
.Pp
Run a 24 GiB single-CPU virtual machine with three network ports, one of which
has a MAC address specified:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bhyve -s 0,hostbridge -s 1,lpc -s 2:0,virtio-net,tap0 \\
  -s 2:1,virtio-net,tap1 \\
  -s 2:2,virtio-net,tap2,mac=00:be:fa:76:45:00 \\
  -s 3,virtio-blk,/my/image -l com1,stdio \\
  -H -P -m 24G bigvm
.Ed
.Pp
Run an 8 GiB quad-CPU virtual machine with 8 AHCI SATA disks, an AHCI ATAPI
CD-ROM, a single virtio network port, an AMD hostbridge, and the console
port connected to an
.Xr nmdm 4
null-modem device.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bhyve -c 4 \\
  -s 0,amd_hostbridge -s 1,lpc \\
  -s 1:0,ahci,hd:/images/disk.1,hd:/images/disk.2,\\
hd:/images/disk.3,hd:/images/disk.4,\\
hd:/images/disk.5,hd:/images/disk.6,\\
hd:/images/disk.7,hd:/images/disk.8,\\
cd:/images/install.iso \\
  -s 3,virtio-net,tap0 \\
  -l com1,/dev/nmdm0A \\
  -H -P -m 8G
.Ed
.Pp
Run a UEFI virtual machine with a display resolution of 800 by 600 pixels
that can be accessed via VNC at: 0.0.0.0:5900 or via serial console over
TCP at: 127.0.0.1:1234 (unsafe if you expose serial console without protection).
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bhyve -c 2 -m 4G -w -H \\
  -s 0,hostbridge \\
  -s 3,ahci-cd,/path/to/uefi-OS-install.iso \\
  -s 4,ahci-hd,disk.img \\
  -s 5,virtio-net,tap0 \\
  -s 29,fbuf,tcp=0.0.0.0:5900,w=800,h=600,wait \\
  -s 30,xhci,tablet \\
  -s 31,lpc -l com1,tcp=127.0.0.1:1234 \\
  -l bootrom,/usr/local/share/uefi-firmware/BHYVE_UEFI.fd \\
   uefivm
.Ed
.Pp
Run a UEFI virtual machine with a VNC display that is bound to all IPv6
addresses on port 5900 and a serial I/O port bound to TCP port 1234 of
loopback address (unsafe if you expose serial console without protection).
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bhyve -c 2 -m 4G -w -H \\
  -s 0,hostbridge \\
  -s 4,ahci-hd,disk.img \\
  -s 5,virtio-net,tap0 \\
  -s 29,fbuf,tcp=[::]:5900,w=800,h=600 \\
  -s 30,xhci,tablet \\
  -s 31,lpc -l com1,tcp=[::1]:1234 \\
  -l bootrom,/usr/local/share/uefi-firmware/BHYVE_UEFI.fd \\
   uefivm
.Ed
.Pp
Run a UEFI virtual machine with a VARS file to save EFI variables.
Note that
.Nm
will write guest modifications to the given VARS file.
Be sure to create a per-guest copy of the template VARS file from
.Pa /usr .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bhyve -c 2 -m 4g -w -H \\
  -s 0,hostbridge \\
  -s 31,lpc -l com1,stdio \\
  -l bootrom,/usr/local/share/uefi-firmware/BHYVE_UEFI_CODE.fd,BHYVE_UEFI_VARS.fd
   uefivm
.Ed
.Pp
To create a configuration file
.Pa configfile
for a virtual machine, use
.Fl o
.Ar config.dump=1 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/usr/sbin/bhyve -c 2 -m 256 -H -P \\
  -s 0:0,hostbridge -s 1:0,virtio-net,tap0 \\
  -s 2:0,ahci-hd,./vm0.img \\
  -s 31,lpc -l com1,stdio \\
  -o config.dump=1 vm0 > configfile
.Ed
.Pp
Then use an editor of your choice to remove the line "config.dump=1"
from the newly generated
.Pa configfile .
.Pp
To start
.Nm
using this configuration file, use flag
.Fl k :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/usr/sbin/bhyve -k configfile vm0
.Ed
.Pp
Run a UEFI virtual machine with four CPUs and two emulated NUMA domains:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bhyve -c 4 -w -H \\
  -s 0,hostbridge \\
  -s 4,ahci-hd,disk.img \\
  -s 31,lpc -l com1,stdio \\
  -l bootrom,/usr/local/share/uefi-firmware/BHYVE_UEFI.fd \\
  -n id=0,size=4G,cpus=0-1 \\
  -n id=1,size=4G,cpus=2-3 \\
   numavm
.Ed
.Pp
Assuming a host machine with two NUMA domains,
run a UEFI virtual machine with four CPUs using a
.Ar prefer
.Xr domainset 9
policy to allocate guest memory from the first host NUMA domain only.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
bhyve -c 2 -w -H \\
  -s 0,hostbridge \\
  -s 4,ahci-hd,disk.img \\
  -s 31,lpc -l com1,stdio \\
  -l bootrom,/usr/local/share/uefi-firmware/BHYVE_UEFI.fd \\
  -n id=0,size=4G,cpus=0-1,domain_policy=prefer:0 \\
   numavm
.Ed
